  "hints": [
    "How does the binary form of i relate to the binary form of i >> 1 (i halved)?",
    "i >> 1 drops the lowest bit; that bit is i & 1.",
    "counts[i] = counts[i >> 1] + (i & 1), filled from 1 upwards."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function bitCounts(n) {\n  const counts = new Array(n + 1).fill(0)\n  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) counts[i] = counts[i >> 1] + (i & 1)\n  return counts\n}\n",
    "py": "def bit_counts(n):\n    counts = [0] * (n + 1)\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        counts[i] = counts[i >> 1] + (i & 1)\n    return counts\n",
    "explanation": "Shifting right by one removes exactly the lowest bit, so i has the same number of 1 bits as i >> 1, plus one if its lowest bit is set. Since i >> 1 < i, that answer is already known."
  },
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n)",
    "space": "O(n) for the output",
    "notes": "Another recurrence: counts[i] = counts[i & (i - 1)] + 1, because i & (i - 1) clears the lowest set bit."
  },
  "followUps": [
